This Field Agent Operations page is the field officer’s working console. It is used when NATCODEV sends a field agent to physically verify a farmer’s farm, capture field evidence, and report observations back into the platform.

What It Does

The field agent can see assigned farm tasks, like the Test Coconut Farm assigned to Test Grower.

For each farm visit, the agent can:

Capture the real visit GPS coordinates.
Compare the agent’s location with the farmer’s submitted farm location.
Mark the farm as Verified on site, Needs admin review, Rejected, or Submit evidence only.
Add field notes about access, crop condition, boundary evidence, and farmer interview.
Record agronomy observations like crop symptoms, pest signs, weed pressure, water stress, and soil condition.
Submit the visit into the system for admin/governance review.
The Offline Sync Center means the agent can still work in areas with poor network. Visits can be saved offline, queued, and synced later when internet returns.

The Visit Map shows field visit locations.

The Certificate Verification section lets the agent verify certificate/application references while in the field.

The Offline Resources section gives the agent access to training guides or field manuals.

Benefit To The Farmer

It protects the farmer from being wrongly rejected based only on desk review.

A farmer benefits because:

Their farm can be physically verified.
GPS evidence supports their application.
Field notes can explain issues that online forms cannot capture.
Agronomy problems can be reported early.
The farmer’s documents, farm, and certificate journey become more credible.
If internet is bad in the farmer’s area, the visit is not wasted because the agent can sync later.
Benefit To Governance

It gives NATCODEV stronger control, auditability, and fraud prevention.

Governance benefits because:

Admins can verify that a farm actually exists.
GPS distance helps detect false or inaccurate farm locations.
Every visit creates an audit trail: agent, farm, result, notes, date, and coordinates.
Offline sync prevents field-data loss.
Agronomy observations can trigger advisory or follow-up cases.
Certificates and approvals become evidence-backed, not just form-based.
State coordinators and admins can monitor field activity and verification progress.
In simple terms: this page turns NATCODEV from “people filled a form online” into “we verified the farm, saw the conditions, captured location evidence, and created a record government/admin can trust.”
The agent captures GPS coordinates by clicking “Use Current GPS” on the assigned field task.

What happens:

The browser asks the device for its current location.
The phone/laptop GPS provides the agent’s latitude and longitude.
The system fills these two fields automatically:
Visit latitude
Visit longitude
The agent then clicks Submit Visit.
NATCODEV saves those coordinates with the field visit record.
So the agent should do this while physically standing at or near the farmer’s farm.

Important: the browser/device must allow location access. On a phone, GPS/location services must be turned on. On desktop, the browser may use Wi-Fi/network location, which is less accurate than phone GPS.